  "worked_solution": "The intersection of a family of indexed sets ${{A}_{i}}_{i\\in I}$ contains all elements that belong to *all* sets in our family:\n\n$$\n{\u22c2}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for all\u00a0}{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nThe notation ${\u22c2}_{i=1}^{\\infty }$ means that index set is \n$N={1,2,3\u2026}.$ \n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=1,$ we have ${X}_{1}={0,1,2}$\n\n- if $i=2,$ we have ${X}_{2}={0,2,4}$\n\n- if $i=3,$ we have ${X}_{3}={0,3,6}$\n\n- $\u22ef$\n\nand so on. \n\nNotice that the only element that lies in all the sets is $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u22c2}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{X}_{i}={0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
